CertHub.register({"id":"google-cdl","vendor":"Google Cloud","name":"Google Cloud Certified Cloud Digital Leader","short":"Cloud Digital Leader","exam":"Cloud Digital Leader","blurb":"Entry-level Google Cloud certification for anyone who works with cloud projects, technical or not: the business value of cloud, and how Google Cloud products for data, AI, infrastructure, security and operations support digital transformation.","status":"check","statusNote":"Google Cloud's websites could not be opened from this environment, so nothing was confirmed on the official page during this task. The six sections and their weights (Digital transformation with Google Cloud 17%; Exploring data transformation with Google Cloud 16%; Innovating with Google Cloud artificial intelligence 16%; Modernize infrastructure and applications with Google Cloud 17%; Trust and security with Google Cloud 17%; Scaling with Google Cloud operations 17%) come from the published Cloud Digital Leader exam guide as best known. The exam length, format and price are also as best known. Confirm the sections, weights and exam details on the official certification page and exam guide before booking.","lastVerified":"2026-09-25","notices":[],"examInfo":{"questions":"About 50–60 multiple choice and multiple select (as best known; confirm on the exam page)","minutes":90,"pass":"Not published","extra":"No prerequisites and no hands-on experience required; the exam is aimed at business and technical roles alike. It can be taken online-proctored or at a test center. The certification is valid for three years (as best known)."},"examSim":{"questions":60,"minutes":90},"sources":[{"label":"Google Cloud: Cloud Digital Leader certification page (exam guide linked from it)","url":"https://cloud.google.com/learn/certification/cloud-digital-leader"}],"planWeeks":6,"hoursPerWeek":"4–6","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Digital transformation with Google Cloud","w":17,"topics":["What cloud computing is and why it drives digital transformation","Business benefits of the cloud: scalability, elasticity, agility, reliability and strategic value","CapEx vs OpEx and total cost of ownership (TCO) when moving to the cloud","Deployment options: on-premises, private cloud, public cloud, hybrid cloud and multicloud","Cloud service models: IaaS, PaaS, SaaS and serverless, and what the customer manages in each","The shared responsibility model and how it changes with the service model","Google Cloud global infrastructure: regions, zones, edge points of presence and Google's private network","Network performance basics: bandwidth, latency and choosing locations close to users","Open source, open standards and avoiding vendor lock-in","Leading digital transformation: culture, skills and change management in a cloud adoption"],"notes":["Exam guide section 1: why cloud technology is transforming business, fundamental cloud concepts, cloud computing models and shared responsibility"],"labs":["No-cost: open the Google Cloud Pricing Calculator, price one small VM running 24x7 for a month, then price the same VM for 8 hours a day on weekdays only. Write two sentences on how pay-as-you-go changes the cost compared with buying a server up front.","Free tier: create a Google Cloud account (new accounts get free trial credits; Always Free products stay free within their limits), open the console and Cloud Shell, and run `gcloud compute regions list` and `gcloud compute zones list`. Note three regions and their zones. Listing locations creates nothing and costs nothing.","No-cost: take one application you use at work or school and write down who manages each layer (facilities, hardware, OS, runtime, application, data, access) if it ran on-premises, on IaaS, on PaaS and as SaaS. Compare your table with the shared responsibility lesson."]},{"id":2,"name":"Exploring data transformation with Google Cloud","w":16,"topics":["Why data matters: using data to drive decisions, products and innovation","Structured, semi-structured and unstructured data","Databases, data warehouses and data lakes: what each is for","Cloud Storage and its storage classes: Standard, Nearline, Coldline and Archive","Relational databases on Google Cloud: Cloud SQL, AlloyDB and Spanner","Non-relational databases on Google Cloud: Firestore and Bigtable","BigQuery: serverless data warehouse and analytics","Streaming and processing data: Pub/Sub, Dataflow and Dataproc","Business intelligence with Looker and Looker Studio","Moving data to Google Cloud: Database Migration Service, BigQuery Data Transfer Service, Storage Transfer Service and Transfer Appliance","Data governance: quality, security, access control and cataloging"],"notes":["Exam guide section 2: the value of data, Google Cloud data management solutions, and making data useful and accessible"],"labs":["Free (BigQuery sandbox, no credit card needed): open BigQuery in the console, query a public dataset such as `bigquery-public-data.usa_names.usa_1910_current` to find the ten most common names in one state, and look at the bytes processed before running. Stay well inside the free monthly query allowance and use LIMIT or selected columns rather than SELECT *.","Free tier: create a Cloud Storage bucket in a US region with the Standard class, upload a small text file, then change the object's storage class to Nearline and read the minimum storage duration shown in the docs. Delete the object and the bucket when you finish so nothing is billed.","Free: connect Looker Studio to the BigQuery public dataset query from the first lab (or to a Google Sheet) and build a one-page dashboard with a table, a bar chart and a filter. Share it view-only with a friend."]},{"id":3,"name":"Innovating with Google Cloud artificial intelligence","w":16,"topics":["Artificial intelligence, machine learning and generative AI: definitions and differences","Business problems machine learning can solve, and when ML is not the right tool","Data quality for machine learning: accuracy, completeness, representativeness and bias","Responsible AI: Google's AI Principles, fairness, explainability, privacy and accountability","Pre-trained AI APIs: Vision, Natural Language, Speech-to-Text, Text-to-Speech and Translation","BigQuery ML: building and using models with SQL where the data already lives","Vertex AI: the unified ML platform, AutoML and custom training","Choosing an AI approach: pre-trained API, BigQuery ML, AutoML or custom model","Generative AI on Google Cloud: Gemini models, Vertex AI Model Garden and Vertex AI Studio","Grounding, agents and AI-powered search and conversation for business","AI infrastructure: GPUs and Tensor Processing Units (TPUs)"],"notes":["Exam guide section 3: AI and ML fundamentals, Google Cloud's AI and ML solutions, and choosing the right solution"],"labs":["Free: try the Cloud Vision API demo page (drag in a photo without people or private information) and note the labels, detected text and SafeSearch results. Write down which business process could use each output.","Free (BigQuery sandbox): follow the BigQuery ML beginner tutorial to train a small logistic regression model on a public dataset with `CREATE MODEL`, then run `ML.EVALUATE` and `ML.PREDICT`. Keep the dataset small so training and queries stay within the free allowance, and delete the model afterward.","Free trial or no-cost access: open Vertex AI Studio (or the Gemini app) and write the same business prompt three ways: plain, with a role and format, and with a short example. Compare the answers and list one risk (hallucination, sensitive data) you would control before using it at work. Set a budget alert first if you use a billing account."]},{"id":4,"name":"Modernize infrastructure and applications with Google Cloud","w":17,"topics":["Why modernize: benefits of moving infrastructure and applications to the cloud","Migration approaches: retire, retain, rehost (lift and shift), replatform, refactor and reimagine","Virtual machines with Compute Engine: machine types, managed instance groups and autoscaling","Keeping existing platforms: Google Cloud VMware Engine and Bare Metal Solution","Containers: what they are and why they make applications portable","Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE): managed Kubernetes in Standard and Autopilot modes","Serverless computing: Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions and App Engine","Choosing compute for a workload: VMs vs containers vs serverless","Monoliths vs microservices and application modernization","APIs and API management with Apigee","Hybrid and multicloud with GKE Enterprise (formerly Anthos)"],"notes":["Exam guide section 4: modernizing infrastructure in the cloud, modernizing applications, and the value of APIs, hybrid and multicloud"],"labs":["Free tier: in Cloud Shell, deploy the sample 'hello' container to Cloud Run with `gcloud run deploy`, open the URL, then look at the revisions and the request metrics. Cloud Run scales to zero when idle, and the free monthly request allowance covers this; delete the service when done.","Free tier: create one e2-micro VM in an Always Free eligible US region with a standard persistent disk, SSH in from the console, run `uptime`, then stop and delete it. Always check the current free-tier regions and delete the VM and its disk so nothing is billed.","No-cost: pick three applications (for example a legacy payroll system, a company blog and a new mobile backend) and choose a migration approach (retire, retain, rehost, replatform, refactor, reimagine) and a compute option (Compute Engine, GKE, Cloud Run) for each, with one sentence of reasoning."]},{"id":5,"name":"Trust and security with Google Cloud","w":17,"topics":["Core security concepts: confidentiality, integrity, availability, privacy, control and compliance","Cloud security vs on-premises security, and shared responsibility for security","Common cloud threats: misconfiguration, compromised credentials, phishing, malware and ransomware","Zero trust and defense in depth","Google's secure infrastructure: data centers, custom hardware, Titan chips and the private network","Encryption at rest and in transit, and key management with Cloud KMS","Identity and access management: principals, roles, least privilege and two-step verification","Network and perimeter security: firewall rules, Cloud Armor and VPC Service Controls","Security operations: Security Command Center, audit logs and Google Security Operations","Data residency, data sovereignty and Assured Workloads","Compliance and transparency: compliance reports, Access Transparency and Google's trust principles"],"notes":["Exam guide section 5: trust and security in the cloud, Google's trusted infrastructure, and Google Cloud's trust principles and compliance"],"labs":["Free: in a Google Cloud project, open IAM and look at the roles granted. Add a second Google account as Viewer on the project only, sign in as that account and confirm it can see but not change resources, then remove the grant. IAM itself is free.","Free: turn on 2-Step Verification for the Google account you use with Google Cloud (preferably with a security key or authenticator app) and write down how you would recover access if you lost the device.","Free: open Logging > Logs Explorer and filter for `logName:\"cloudaudit.googleapis.com%2Factivity\"` to see Admin Activity audit log entries from the IAM change you made in the first lab. Note who made the change, when and from where. Admin Activity audit logs are always on and free."]},{"id":6,"name":"Scaling with Google Cloud operations","w":17,"topics":["Cloud financial governance and FinOps: shared accountability for cloud cost","The Google Cloud resource hierarchy: organization, folders, projects and resources","Policy inheritance: IAM allow policies and organization policies through the hierarchy","Controlling costs: billing accounts, budgets and alerts, quotas, labels and billing export","Pricing models and discounts: pay-as-you-go, sustained use discounts, committed use discounts and Spot VMs","DevOps and Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) principles","SLIs, SLOs, SLAs and error budgets","Reliability and disaster recovery: redundancy across zones and regions, backups, RTO and RPO","Google Cloud Observability: Cloud Monitoring, Cloud Logging, Cloud Trace and Error Reporting","Google Cloud Customer Care: support plans and when to use them","Sustainability: Google's carbon-free energy goals, low-carbon regions and the Carbon Footprint tool"],"notes":["Exam guide section 6: financial governance and managing cloud costs, operational excellence and reliability at scale, and sustainability with Google Cloud"],"labs":["Free: in Billing > Budgets & alerts, create a budget of a small amount (for example 5 in your currency) on your billing account with alerts at 50%, 90% and 100%. Note that a budget sends alerts but does not stop spending on its own.","Free: in your project, add labels such as `env=lab` and `team=study` to a Cloud Storage bucket, then open Billing reports and group costs by label. Also open IAM & Admin > Quotas and find the quota for one product. Delete the bucket afterward.","Free: in Cloud Monitoring, create an uptime check against a public page you own or the Cloud Run service from the domain 4 lab, and an alerting policy that emails you if it fails. Then open the Carbon Footprint page in the console and read which region your usage came from. Delete the uptime check and service when done."]}],"study":{"1":[["What does digital transformation mean, and how does the cloud enable it?","Digital transformation is using new digital technology to change how an organization works, serves customers and creates value, not just moving servers. The cloud enables it with on-demand resources, managed services, global reach and pay-as-you-go pricing, so teams can experiment and scale quickly."],["Contrast CapEx and OpEx in a cloud move.","Capital expenditure (CapEx) is up-front spending on assets such as servers and data centers that are depreciated over years. Operating expenditure (OpEx) is ongoing spending for what you use. Cloud shifts most IT spending from CapEx to OpEx and lowers the risk of buying capacity you never use."],["Explain IaaS, PaaS and SaaS with a Google Cloud or Google example of each.","IaaS gives you raw compute, storage and networks and you manage the OS and above (Compute Engine). PaaS gives you a platform where you bring code and data (App Engine, Cloud Run). SaaS is a finished application you simply use (Google Workspace)."],["What is the difference between a region and a zone?","A region is an independent geographic area, such as us-central1. A zone is an isolated deployment area within a region, such as us-central1-a. Spreading across zones protects against a single zone failure; spreading across regions protects against a regional outage and helps serve users in other places."],["What is the difference between hybrid cloud and multicloud?","Hybrid cloud combines on-premises (or private cloud) infrastructure with a public cloud. Multicloud uses more than one public cloud provider. An organization can be both at once."],["Why does Google emphasize open source and open standards?","Open technologies such as Kubernetes, TensorFlow and Apache Beam let workloads run in many places, reduce vendor lock-in and let customers use familiar tools and skills."]],"2":[["Compare a database, a data warehouse and a data lake.","A database stores current operational data for an application (for example orders). A data warehouse stores cleaned, structured data from many sources for analytics and reporting (BigQuery). A data lake stores large amounts of raw data in any format for later processing (often Cloud Storage)."],["Name the four Cloud Storage classes and when to use each.","Standard for frequently accessed (hot) data; Nearline for data read about once a month or less (30-day minimum); Coldline for data read about once a quarter (90-day minimum); Archive for data read less than once a year, such as long-term backups and compliance copies (365-day minimum)."],["When would you choose Spanner over Cloud SQL?","When a relational workload needs strong consistency and horizontal scale beyond one machine or across regions, such as a global inventory or financial ledger. Cloud SQL is managed MySQL, PostgreSQL or SQL Server for regional workloads of normal size."],["Firestore or Bigtable?","Firestore is a serverless document database for web and mobile apps, with real-time updates and offline support. Bigtable is a wide-column NoSQL database for very large, high-throughput, low-latency workloads such as time series, IoT and ad tech."],["How do Pub/Sub and Dataflow work together?","Pub/Sub ingests streams of events from many publishers and delivers them asynchronously to subscribers. Dataflow, a managed Apache Beam service, reads from Pub/Sub, transforms and enriches the data in real time and writes it to destinations such as BigQuery."],["What is the difference between Looker and Looker Studio?","Looker is an enterprise BI platform with a governed semantic model (LookML) so everyone uses the same metric definitions. Looker Studio is a free, self-service tool for building dashboards and reports from many data sources."]],"3":[["How do AI, machine learning and generative AI relate?","AI is the broad field of making machines perform tasks that need human-like intelligence. ML is a subset where systems learn patterns from data instead of following hand-written rules. Generative AI is a type of ML that creates new content such as text, images or code."],["Why does data quality matter so much in ML?","A model learns only from its training data. Inaccurate, incomplete, out-of-date or unrepresentative data produces poor or biased predictions, however good the algorithm."],["Contrast pre-trained APIs, AutoML and custom training.","Pre-trained APIs (Vision, Translation) give instant results with no training for common tasks. AutoML trains a model on your own labeled data with little ML expertise. Custom training gives data scientists full control of the code and model for unique, differentiating problems but needs the most skill and time."],["What is BigQuery ML good for?","Letting analysts create, train and run ML models with SQL directly in BigQuery, so data does not have to be moved and people who know SQL can build forecasts, classifications and recommendations."],["What does 'grounding' mean for generative AI?","Connecting a model's answers to trusted sources, such as your company documents or search results, so responses are based on real, current information and hallucinations are reduced."],["What are TPUs?","Tensor Processing Units are Google's custom-designed chips (ASICs) that accelerate machine learning training and inference, available in Google Cloud alongside GPUs."]],"4":[["Contrast rehost, replatform and refactor.","Rehost (lift and shift) moves an application as-is, usually onto VMs, fastest but with the least cloud benefit. Replatform makes small changes to use managed services, for example moving the database to Cloud SQL. Refactor changes the code and architecture, for example to microservices, for the most benefit and effort."],["What is a managed instance group?","A group of identical Compute Engine VMs created from an instance template that Google can autoscale, autoheal (recreate unhealthy VMs) and update in a rolling way, often behind a load balancer."],["GKE Standard vs Autopilot?","In Standard mode you manage and pay for the cluster's nodes. In Autopilot, Google manages the nodes and infrastructure and you pay for the resources your pods request, which reduces operational work."],["When is Cloud Run a good fit?","For stateless containerized web apps, APIs and jobs that should scale automatically, including to zero, with no servers or clusters to manage and billing based on use."],["What does Apigee add on top of APIs?","API management: security (keys, OAuth), rate limiting and quotas, analytics, versioning, a developer portal and monetization, so a company can safely expose its data and services to partners and developers."],["What problem does GKE Enterprise solve?","Consistent management of Kubernetes clusters and policies across Google Cloud, on-premises data centers and other clouds from one place, which supports hybrid and multicloud strategies."]],"5":[["What is the shared responsibility model for security?","Google secures the underlying infrastructure (data centers, hardware, network, and more of the stack in managed services). Customers are always responsible for their data, who can access it and how they configure services. The customer's share is largest in IaaS and smallest in SaaS."],["What is zero trust?","A security model that trusts no user or device by default because of its network location. Every request is verified using identity, device state and context, and access is least privilege. Google's BeyondCorp is an implementation of it."],["How does Google Cloud encrypt data?","All customer data is encrypted at rest by default with Google-managed keys and encrypted in transit between Google facilities. Customers who need more control can use customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) in Cloud KMS, hardware-backed keys, or keys held outside Google."],["What is the principle of least privilege?","Give each identity only the permissions it needs for its job, for only as long as it needs them. In Google Cloud that means using narrow predefined or custom roles on the smallest resource scope rather than broad basic roles like Owner or Editor."],["Data residency vs data sovereignty?","Data residency is the physical location where data is stored, controlled by choosing regions. Data sovereignty is the legal principle that data is subject to the laws of the country where it is located or of its owner; it also covers who can access it and under which laws."],["What do Access Transparency and Access Approval provide?","Access Transparency gives near real-time logs when Google personnel access customer content and why. Access Approval lets customers explicitly approve or deny such access before it happens."]],"6":[["Describe the resource hierarchy.","Organization (the company's root node) contains folders (departments or teams, which can nest), which contain projects, which contain resources such as VMs and buckets. Projects are the basis for enabling APIs, billing and grouping resources."],["How are policies inherited?","IAM allow policies and organization policies set at a node apply to everything below it. IAM access is additive: a lower level can grant more but cannot remove access granted above it. This is why broad roles should be granted high only with care."],["Do budgets stop spending?","No. A Cloud Billing budget sends alerts at thresholds you choose (and can notify Pub/Sub for automated responses). To cap usage you use quotas or build automation that reacts to the budget notifications."],["What is the difference between an SLI, SLO and SLA?","An SLI is a measurement of service behavior, such as the percentage of successful requests. An SLO is the internal target for that measurement, such as 99.9% over 30 days. An SLA is a contract with customers that states consequences, such as credits, if a level is not met."],["What is an error budget?","The amount of unreliability an SLO allows (100% minus the SLO). While budget remains, teams can release features; when it is used up, they focus on reliability. It balances innovation with stability."],["What are RTO and RPO?","Recovery time objective is how long a service can be down before it must be restored. Recovery point objective is how much data, measured in time, the business can afford to lose. Smaller values need more expensive DR designs."]]},"qCount":90,"hasLessons":true,"hasPbqs":true});
